    champ_note: "And if we judge <b>dishonestly</b> — by fitting to the whole chart? Here are the “champions” and what they delivered blind. By raw return the fitting leaders are <b>Bollinger breakout +234%</b> (daily) and <b>Donchian channel +204%</b> (hourly); by risk (Sharpe) — the cards below.",
    surface_note: "This is a “fitting map” for the moving-average cross: each cell is a (fast, slow) pair, the colour is its Sharpe over the whole history. Fitting = pick the brightest cell <i>knowing the whole chart</i>. But neighbouring cells are often far worse — so the choice is fragile, built on luck rather than a pattern.",
    folds_plain: "This is what blind trading looks like step by step. On each window the “expectation” column is the Sharpe the parameters showed on the training (past) data, and “reality” is what they delivered on the next, unseen slice. The systematic shortfall of reality below expectation is the measure of overfitting.",
    daytrade_note: "“Day trading” here means strategies on <b>hourly</b> candles with frequent trades. Note: most of them <b>lose money</b> out of sample — fees and noise eat the profit. Only trend breakout systems survive. That is the typical fate of intraday trading.",
    weekly_note: "“Medium-term” means strategies on <b>daily</b> candles. They make about one trade every one to two weeks, matching “a trade roughly once a week”. Here trend strategies (especially MACD) beat buy & hold on return with half the risk.",
